1
00:00:00,590 --> 00:00:06,110
When people use the word common sense, what they mean is uncommon sense, because the standard

2
00:00:06,110 --> 00:00:08,430
human condition is ignorance and stupidity.

3
00:00:08,430 --> 00:00:09,910
It really is.

4
00:00:09,910 --> 00:00:13,770
The standard human condition is a lot of miscognition.

5
00:00:13,770 --> 00:00:18,350
You can improve your own life by eliminating your miscognitions.

6
00:00:18,350 --> 00:00:21,550
You know the economy sometimes booms and sometimes it doesn't.

7
00:00:21,550 --> 00:00:25,030
And you have to live your life through both episodes.

8
00:00:25,030 --> 00:00:29,550
And our attitude is we just keep swimming and sometimes the tide is with us and

9
00:00:29,550 --> 00:00:30,870
sometimes against.

10
00:00:30,870 --> 00:00:32,950
But we keep swimming either way.

11
00:00:32,950 --> 00:00:35,990
Are you surprised by how long this expansion has lasted?

12
00:00:35,990 --> 00:00:37,870
Of course it's lasted a long time.

13
00:00:37,870 --> 00:00:43,190
But what was really remarkable is we never printed money so much and spent it so fast

14
00:00:43,190 --> 00:00:45,750
and bought back so much debt, public and private.

15
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:49,990
So this is total terra incognita in economics.

16
00:00:49,990 --> 00:00:52,230
And nobody knew for sure how it was going to work.

17
00:00:52,230 --> 00:00:54,630
Of course it was risky, but it worked.

18
00:00:54,630 --> 00:00:57,350
I don't think they had much else that would work.

19
00:00:57,350 --> 00:00:59,750
They weren't set up to do stimulus.

20
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:04,710
Too much controversy, democratic inertia, so they had to do something.

21
00:01:04,710 --> 00:01:08,190
And all they had left was just to print money and start buying things.

22
00:01:08,190 --> 00:01:09,190
And that's what they did.

23
00:01:09,190 --> 00:01:13,190
And it turned out to be a very wise response.

24
00:01:13,190 --> 00:01:18,470
And what's even more remarkable is that both Congress and the presidency and

25
00:01:18,470 --> 00:01:20,630
their both parties made the same decision.

26
00:01:20,630 --> 00:01:21,630
They all cooperated.

27
00:01:21,630 --> 00:01:22,630
It was the last time.

28
00:01:22,630 --> 00:01:25,590
Well, it left us licking the Great Recession.

29
00:01:25,590 --> 00:01:30,270
So maybe we ought to try cooperation again since it worked so well once.

30
00:01:30,270 --> 00:01:34,590
If you're a politician in a democracy, of course you want people to print money and

31
00:01:34,590 --> 00:01:35,590
spend it.

32
00:01:35,590 --> 00:01:38,150
And of course it's not a good idea.

33
00:01:38,150 --> 00:01:44,510
The best example, probably in the whole world, is Singapore, which has zero debt.

34
00:01:44,510 --> 00:01:46,870
And never prints money and spends it.

35
00:01:46,870 --> 00:01:49,350
And it's one of the most successful places on earth.

36
00:01:49,350 --> 00:01:52,870
I wish we were like that, but there's only one Singapore.

37
00:01:52,870 --> 00:01:56,470
Will some people now say that federal debt is not a problem at all?

38
00:01:56,470 --> 00:01:59,210
Well, if you believe that, you believe in the tooth fairy.

39
00:01:59,210 --> 00:02:01,770
Because then we don't have to have any more taxes ever.

40
00:02:01,770 --> 00:02:04,410
We'll just print money and live happily ever after.

41
00:02:04,410 --> 00:02:06,330
It obviously won't work.

42
00:02:06,330 --> 00:02:09,450
There comes a point when printing money is counterproductive.

43
00:02:09,450 --> 00:02:10,450
Are we at that point?

44
00:02:10,450 --> 00:02:11,450
Are you concerned?

45
00:02:11,450 --> 00:02:12,450
No, I don't think we are at that point.

46
00:02:12,450 --> 00:02:15,250
But nobody knew where the point was going to come.

47
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:16,250
And we don't know now.

48
00:02:16,250 --> 00:02:20,850
None of these people who are so pompously sure of things, because we all want

49
00:02:20,850 --> 00:02:23,250
reassurance until they provide it.

50
00:02:23,250 --> 00:02:26,890
Nobody really knows how much of this is too much.

51
00:02:26,890 --> 00:02:28,130
It happened by accident.

52
00:02:28,130 --> 00:02:29,130
We were in desperate trouble.

53
00:02:29,130 --> 00:02:32,810
We were on the eve of a great recession that could have been a great depression,

54
00:02:32,810 --> 00:02:37,490
and then followed by the rise of people like Adolf Hitler and so on and so on.

55
00:02:37,490 --> 00:02:39,490
So we faced a real catastrophe.

56
00:02:39,490 --> 00:02:44,330
The only weapon they had with this huge debt was to print money and spend it.

57
00:02:44,330 --> 00:02:45,530
And they did it.

58
00:02:45,530 --> 00:02:48,850
And they'd draw interest rates down to zero, or real interest rates.

59
00:02:48,850 --> 00:02:53,890
Well, of course, that lifted asset values for the people who were already rich.

60
00:02:53,890 --> 00:02:55,690
Nobody was trying to make the rich richer.

61
00:02:55,690 --> 00:03:02,730
It just was an accidental byproduct of a correct governmental decision made on a bipartisan

62
00:03:02,730 --> 00:03:03,730
basis.

63
00:03:03,730 --> 00:03:08,090
And since it was a weird byproduct that occurred in a weird time, it would go away by itself

64
00:03:08,090 --> 00:03:09,610
and do course.

65
00:03:09,610 --> 00:03:14,810
I can't think of a single example in my whole life where keeping it simple has

66
00:03:14,810 --> 00:03:16,210
worked against us.

67
00:03:16,210 --> 00:03:20,090
We made mistakes, but they weren't because we kept it simple.

68
00:03:20,090 --> 00:03:25,570
We're trying to find intelligent things to do with a torrent of surplus cash.

69
00:03:25,570 --> 00:03:28,130
And we've always had a torrent of surplus cash.

70
00:03:28,130 --> 00:03:30,850
And we're always looking for intelligent things to do with it.

71
00:03:30,850 --> 00:03:34,210
And if we find things that are intelligent to do, we do it.

72
00:03:34,210 --> 00:03:37,050
And if we don't find anything, we let the cash build up.

73
00:03:37,050 --> 00:03:39,330
What the hell is wrong with that?

74
00:03:39,330 --> 00:03:44,610
You know, the game in our kind of life is being able to recognize a good idea

75
00:03:44,610 --> 00:03:49,450
when you rarely get it and rarely is presented to you.

76
00:03:49,450 --> 00:03:53,010
And I think that's something you have to prepare for over a long period.

77
00:03:53,010 --> 00:03:56,810
What is the old saying, that opportunity comes to the prepared mind?

78
00:03:56,810 --> 00:04:02,890
And I don't think you can teach people in two minutes how to have a prepared mind.

79
00:04:02,890 --> 00:04:04,290
But that's the game.

80
00:04:04,290 --> 00:04:08,970
Things we learned 40 years ago, though, will help and recognize the next big idea.

81
00:04:08,970 --> 00:04:11,610
And on opportunity costs, going back to that.

82
00:04:11,610 --> 00:04:16,930
The current freshman economics text, which is sweeping the country,

83
00:04:16,930 --> 00:04:18,930
has right in practically the first page.

84
00:04:18,930 --> 00:04:25,010
And it says, all intelligent people should think primarily in terms of opportunity cost.

85
00:04:25,010 --> 00:04:26,730
And that's obviously correct.

86
00:04:26,730 --> 00:04:31,410
But it's very hard to teach business based on opportunity cost.

87
00:04:31,410 --> 00:04:35,410
It's much easier to teach the capital assets pricing model.

88
00:04:35,410 --> 00:04:38,770
Or you could just punch in numbers and outcome numbers.

89
00:04:38,770 --> 00:04:44,250
And therefore, people teach what is easy to teach instead of what is correct to teach.

90
00:04:44,250 --> 00:04:47,010
It reminds me of Einstein's famous saying.

91
00:04:47,010 --> 00:04:52,020
He says, everything should be made as simple as possible, but no more simple.

92
00:04:52,020 --> 00:04:55,420
Write that down.

93
00:04:55,420 --> 00:04:58,340
Actually, I tend to be a follower of Confucius.

94
00:04:58,340 --> 00:05:02,260
And I think this room is full of Confucian values.

95
00:05:02,260 --> 00:05:06,340
You know, if the first law of Confucianism is filial piety,

96
00:05:06,340 --> 00:05:08,580
particularly toward the elderly males,

97
00:05:08,620 --> 00:05:11,180
you can see why I like that system.

98
00:05:11,180 --> 00:05:15,340
Obviously, considerations of cost are important in business.

99
00:05:15,340 --> 00:05:20,380
And obviously, opportunity costs, which is a doctrine of economics,

100
00:05:20,380 --> 00:05:24,220
really a doctrine of life'smanship, are also very important.

101
00:05:24,220 --> 00:05:27,700
And we've always had that kind of basic thinking.

102
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:29,460
Of course, capital isn't free.

103
00:05:29,460 --> 00:05:33,260
And of course, you can figure cost of capital when you're borrowing money,

104
00:05:33,260 --> 00:05:35,340
or at least you can figure cost of loans.

105
00:05:35,340 --> 00:05:40,260
But the theorists had to develop some theory for what equity cost.

106
00:05:40,260 --> 00:05:42,540
And there they just went bonkers.

107
00:05:42,540 --> 00:05:46,420
They said, if you earned 100% on capital because you had some marvelous business,

108
00:05:46,420 --> 00:05:48,540
your cost of capital was 100%.

109
00:05:48,540 --> 00:05:52,980
And therefore, you shouldn't look at any opportunity that delivered allows the 80%.

110
00:05:52,980 --> 00:05:57,020
That is the kind of thinking which came out of the capital assets pricing models

111
00:05:57,020 --> 00:06:00,380
and so forth that I've always considered inanity.

112
00:06:00,380 --> 00:06:02,940
What is Berkshire's cost of capital?

113
00:06:02,940 --> 00:06:04,580
We have this damn capital.

114
00:06:04,620 --> 00:06:07,180
It just keeps multiplying and multiplying.

115
00:06:07,180 --> 00:06:08,740
What is its cost?

116
00:06:08,740 --> 00:06:13,340
You have perfectly good old-fashioned doctrines like opportunity cost.

117
00:06:13,340 --> 00:06:16,620
At any given time, when we consider an investment,

118
00:06:16,620 --> 00:06:21,060
we have to compare it to the best alternative investment we have at that time.

119
00:06:21,060 --> 00:06:24,940
We had perfectly good old-fashioned ideas that are very basic to use,

120
00:06:24,940 --> 00:06:27,380
but they weren't good enough for these modern theorists.

121
00:06:27,380 --> 00:06:31,060
So they invented all this ridiculous mathematics,

122
00:06:31,060 --> 00:06:36,740
which concluded that the companies that made the most money had the highest costs of capital.

123
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:38,860
Well, all I can say is this not for us.

124
00:06:38,860 --> 00:06:42,300
Now, the other half of that question I leave for Mr. Boeber.

125
00:06:42,300 --> 00:06:47,860
What you find, of course, is that the cost of capital is about a quarter percent below

126
00:06:47,860 --> 00:06:51,260
the return promised by any deal that the CEO wants to do.

127
00:06:51,260 --> 00:06:52,820
Very simple.

128
00:06:52,820 --> 00:06:55,260
You know, we have three questions with capital throughout,

129
00:06:55,260 --> 00:06:58,860
leaving aside whether we want to borrow money, which we generally don't want to do.

130
00:06:58,860 --> 00:07:01,980
One is, does it make it more sense to pay it out to the shareholders

131
00:07:01,980 --> 00:07:03,740
than to keep it within the company?

132
00:07:03,740 --> 00:07:05,740
The sub-question on that is if we pay it out,

133
00:07:05,740 --> 00:07:09,220
is it better off to do it by a repurchase or by a dividend?

134
00:07:09,220 --> 00:07:12,020
The test for whether we pay it out in dividends

135
00:07:12,020 --> 00:07:15,180
is can we create more than a dollar of value within the company

136
00:07:15,180 --> 00:07:16,860
with that dollar than paying it out?

137
00:07:16,860 --> 00:07:18,940
And you never know the answer to that,

138
00:07:18,940 --> 00:07:22,260
but so far the answer is judged by results, is yes, we can.

139
00:07:22,260 --> 00:07:26,060
And we think that prospectively we can, but that's a hope on our part,

140
00:07:26,100 --> 00:07:28,860
and it's justified to some extent by past history,

141
00:07:28,860 --> 00:07:30,300
but it's not a certainty.

142
00:07:30,300 --> 00:07:33,460
Once we've crossed that threshold, then do we repurchase stock?

143
00:07:33,460 --> 00:07:36,060
Well, obviously, if you can buy your stock at a significant discount

144
00:07:36,060 --> 00:07:38,980
from conservatively calculated and intrinsic value

145
00:07:38,980 --> 00:07:41,980
and you can buy it in a reasonable quantity, that's a use for capital.

146
00:07:41,980 --> 00:07:44,340
Beyond that, then the question becomes,

147
00:07:44,340 --> 00:07:46,780
if you have the capital and you think you can create more than a dollar,

148
00:07:46,780 --> 00:07:49,460
how do you create the most with the least risk?

149
00:07:49,460 --> 00:07:50,860
And that gets to business risk.

150
00:07:50,860 --> 00:07:53,460
It doesn't get to any calculation of the volatility.

151
00:07:53,460 --> 00:07:57,780
I don't know the risk in seized candy as measured by its stock volatility

152
00:07:57,780 --> 00:08:00,340
because the stock hasn't been outstanding since 1972.

153
00:08:00,340 --> 00:08:03,700
Does that mean I can't determine how risky a business sees this

154
00:08:03,700 --> 00:08:05,620
because we don't have a daily quote on it?

155
00:08:05,620 --> 00:08:07,820
No, I can determine it by looking at the business

156
00:08:07,820 --> 00:08:11,580
and the competitive environment, which it operates and so on.

157
00:08:11,580 --> 00:08:16,100
So once we cross the threshold of deciding

158
00:08:16,100 --> 00:08:20,460
that we can deploy capital so as to create more than a dollar

159
00:08:20,460 --> 00:08:23,700
or a present value for every dollar retained,

160
00:08:23,700 --> 00:08:26,740
then it's just a question of doing the most intelligent thing that you can find.

161
00:08:26,740 --> 00:08:31,820
And that is the cost of every deal we do is measured by the second best deal

162
00:08:31,820 --> 00:08:35,940
that's around or given timing, doing more of some of the things we're already in.

163
00:08:35,940 --> 00:08:38,340
I have listened to cost of capital discussions

164
00:08:38,340 --> 00:08:40,460
at all kinds of corporate board meetings, everything else,

165
00:08:40,460 --> 00:08:43,100
and I've never found anything that made very much sense in it,

166
00:08:43,100 --> 00:08:46,340
except for the fact that it's what they learned in business school

167
00:08:46,340 --> 00:08:48,100
and that's what the consultants talked about.

168
00:08:48,100 --> 00:08:49,980
And most of the board members would nod their head

169
00:08:49,980 --> 00:08:52,380
without knowing what the hell was going on.

170
00:08:52,380 --> 00:08:54,580
And that's been my history with the cost of capital.

171
00:08:54,580 --> 00:08:57,140
Now, moving on to the big ideas,

172
00:08:57,140 --> 00:08:59,860
you know when you've got a big idea and I can't tell you,

173
00:08:59,860 --> 00:09:02,660
you know, exactly what happens within your nervous system

174
00:09:02,660 --> 00:09:04,060
or brain at that time,

175
00:09:04,060 --> 00:09:08,700
but we've had a relatively few big ideas, good ideas over the years.

176
00:09:08,700 --> 00:09:10,460
So I don't know how many you think we've had in aggregate.

177
00:09:10,460 --> 00:09:12,420
I mean, maybe 25 each year.

178
00:09:12,420 --> 00:09:15,580
You took the top 15 out of Berkshire Hathaway.

179
00:09:15,580 --> 00:09:17,420
Most new people wouldn't be here.

180
00:09:17,460 --> 00:09:19,300
So roughly what, every two years?

181
00:09:19,300 --> 00:09:20,220
Yeah, one every year or two.

182
00:09:20,220 --> 00:09:24,700
And sometimes there'll be a bunch of them like in 1973 and four.

183
00:09:24,700 --> 00:09:28,060
But the problem is for us is that big now really means big.

184
00:09:28,060 --> 00:09:30,300
I mean, it has to be billions of dollars

185
00:09:30,300 --> 00:09:32,940
to move the needle very much at Berkshire.

186
00:09:32,940 --> 00:09:36,700
I would say that when I would turn those pages 50 years ago

187
00:09:36,700 --> 00:09:38,060
in the Moody's manuals,

188
00:09:38,060 --> 00:09:40,060
I would know when I had a big idea.

189
00:09:40,060 --> 00:09:41,820
I've got a half a dozen of them

190
00:09:41,820 --> 00:09:44,620
that I keep the Xeroxes from those reports around

191
00:09:44,620 --> 00:09:47,020
from 50 years ago, just because it was so obvious

192
00:09:47,100 --> 00:09:48,100
that they were incredible.

193
00:09:48,100 --> 00:09:50,020
And that happens every now and then.

194
00:09:50,020 --> 00:09:54,340
When I met Rormor Davidson in the January 1951

195
00:09:54,340 --> 00:09:55,940
and he spent four hours or five hours

196
00:09:55,940 --> 00:09:59,340
with me explaining Geico, I knew it was a big idea.

197
00:09:59,340 --> 00:10:02,220
Eight months later, or probably 10 months later,

198
00:10:02,220 --> 00:10:04,340
I wrote an article to the Commercial and Financial

199
00:10:04,340 --> 00:10:05,780
Chronicle on the security I like best.

200
00:10:05,780 --> 00:10:06,700
It was a big idea.

201
00:10:06,700 --> 00:10:08,180
When I found Western Insurance Securities,

202
00:10:08,180 --> 00:10:10,260
I knew it was a big idea.

203
00:10:10,260 --> 00:10:12,100
I couldn't put millions of dollars into it,

204
00:10:12,100 --> 00:10:14,260
but I didn't have millions so it didn't make any difference.

205
00:10:14,260 --> 00:10:15,580
We've seen things subsequently

206
00:10:15,580 --> 00:10:18,980
and we'll see, you know, we have a normal lifespan.

207
00:10:18,980 --> 00:10:21,780
We'll see a few more before we get done.

208
00:10:21,780 --> 00:10:25,160
Actually, I have a real system.

209
00:10:25,160 --> 00:10:27,960
My idea of a truly big idea is one I get

210
00:10:27,960 --> 00:10:29,960
and I call Charlie and he only says no

211
00:10:29,960 --> 00:10:32,600
rather than that's the worst idea I've ever heard of.

212
00:10:32,600 --> 00:10:34,800
If he just says no, it's a hell of an idea.

